## The short version

Build your repo's complete **front door**, merged via your own PR workflow: a README that onboards, LICENSE (chosen and justified), CONTRIBUTING (grown from your git standards), CODE_OF_CONDUCT, issue and PR templates with starter labels, your communication/maintainer norms, and an honest placeholder for the setup guide. Plus 2–3 well-formed seed issues filed against your own templates. The full setup guide comes later, once there's code to set up.

---

## Why this assignment exists

Every stranger who ever encounters your project — a potential contributor, a curious user, a capstone faculty member, a recruiter — meets it through the same few files, in the same order, in about ninety seconds: *What is this? Can I run it? May I use it? How do I help? What's it like here?* The infrastructure package is you **designing that encounter** instead of leaving it to chance. It's the governance you chose in Weeks 2 and 8, the workflow you set in Week 7, and the welcome you intend — all made concrete enough that a stranger can act on it without ever talking to you.

The transferable lesson is one of the biggest in the course: **writing things down is how you scale yourself.** Documented processes answer questions before they're asked, make expectations enforceable without being personal, and let a project outgrow its founder's availability — which is the only way anything lasts. That's true of open-source repos, of teams inside companies, and of most organizations you'll ever help run. And the honest test of documentation isn't the author's satisfaction — it's whether a cold reader succeeds. That's why your review partner reads your front door cold in the workshop, and why a stranger reads it again, for a grade, in the Week-11 repo audit.

---

## What to produce (all merged to main by the deadline)

These eight items come from the course's required repository artifacts. The same list is also a printable checklist, with a punch-list area for tracking what's left: <file path="../../resources/CP7-Checklist.pdf" canvas_folder="Course Handouts" />

1. **README.md** — one-line what + why → status → quickstart (or, until the project runs, an honest line pointing to the setup placeholder) → how to contribute (link) → license line.
2. **LICENSE** — the exact text, unmodified except for the fields the license itself asks you to fill in. MIT and BSD have them (year and copyright holder); Apache-2.0 and the GPL don't, and Apache's `[yyyy] [name of copyright owner]` line is a template for file headers that stays as written. Record the project's one-paragraph rationale in CONTRIBUTING or a docs note.
3. **CONTRIBUTING.md** — grown from your git-standards doc: a welcome and what a newcomer should do first, then how to pick work, the PR process, and review expectations, with policy last. For setup, link to `docs/setup.md` rather than repeating it, so there's one place to keep current.
4. **CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md** — adopt a standard ([Contributor Covenant](https://www.contributor-covenant.org/version/3/0/code_of_conduct/) is the usual choice), with the reporting contact and enforcement filled in for your project. The contact is public and has to work:
   - **An address you'll check and are willing to publish.** It doesn't have to be your personal one; a BYU address or one created for the project is fine.
   - **How soon reports are read.** It's a promise like any other, so make it one you'll keep.
   - **A route for a report about the maintainer.** On a one-person project that's you, so point to someone else who can act: a co-maintainer, or [GitHub's reporting tools](https://docs.github.com/en/communities/maintaining-your-safety-on-github/reporting-abuse-or-spam).
5. **Issue templates + PR template + starter labels** (`.github/…`) — the bug-report anatomy (steps, expected vs. actual, environment) and feature-request shape built in; the PR template carries the checklist from your git standards (what to link, what to test, what the author confirms before asking for review); labels including `bug`, `enhancement`, `good first issue`. *If your forms apply labels (a `needs triage` label is a common one, below), create the labels first: a form skips any label the repository doesn't have, without a warning.*
6. **Setup guide placeholder** (`docs/setup.md`) — the stack and prerequisites your design document already commits to, a line saying setup instructions arrive with the first runnable build, and a link to the issue that tracks writing them (it can be one of your seed issues). *The full guide is written and cold-tested in Week 10, once there's something to run, and a stranger follows it in the Week-11 repo audit.*
7. **Communication & maintainer norms** (`docs/communication-norms.md`) — where discussion happens, realistic response expectations, who maintains, and how decisions get made. *Solo, that last one still needs an answer: when a contributor proposes something, how is it decided, and where?* A response time is a promise, so say how you'll keep it: **how will you know which new issues are still waiting for an answer?** Projects do this in different ways: a label the issue forms apply and a maintainer removes after reading (Prop Loft's `needs triage`), a *New* column on a project board, or a person who reads everything new on a fixed day. Pick one that fits your project and say in a sentence what it is.
8. **2–3 seed issues** filed on your own tracker, following your own templates — real next-work items. Before there's code, most of them are features or tasks rather than bugs. A task that fits neither form (like writing the setup guide) uses the closest one, or you add a third form for tasks.

